## Chapter 5 V0 Analysis

A V0 is characterised by an electrically neutral particle decaying into two oppositely charged particles, which will bend in opposite directions in the presence of a magnetic field. Unlike the charge neutral parent, the charged daughters may be detected with the TPC. The \(\Lambda\), \(\bar{\Lambda}\), and \(K^{0}_{S}\) V0s relevant to this analysis, most commonly weak decay via the following modes (percentages denote the branching ratios) [7]:

\[\Lambda \to p^{+}+\pi^{-}(62.4\%) \tag{5.1}\] \[\bar{\Lambda} \to p^{-}+\pi^{+}(62.4\%)\] (5.2) \[K^{0}_{S} \rightarrow\pi^{+}+\pi^{-}(69.2\%) \tag{5.3}\]

This chapter will outline the methods used to extracted \(\Lambda\), \(\bar{\Lambda}\), and \(K^{0}_{S}\) yields as a function of \(p_{T}\) and centrality from Cu+Cu \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=200\) GeV collisions. Firstly, background reduction and raw signal extraction will be described, and this will be followed by the methods used to correct the raw yields for V0 detection inefficiencies. For the \(\Lambda\), \(\bar{\Lambda}\) particles, the secondary yield contribution from \(\Xi\) weak decays will then be determined, and the chapter will finish with systematic uncertainty determination1.

### 5.1 V0 Finding

For a given event, V0 finding starts with identification of all possible pairs of oppositely charged global tracks2. Prior to this information being written into the MuDst data file, default cuts are imposed on various topologies in order to reduce background from random track crossings. Figure 5.1 shows the topology of a V0, and the corresponding default cuts are in table 5.1. These will be explained as follows.

Footnote 2: V0 finding uses the standard STAR software [99]

Firstly, as most primary track crossings occur close to the primary vertex, the decay length cut of 2 cm will reject these. The cuts on the distance of closest approach of the V0 daughters, and the distance of closest approach of the V0 to the primary vertex, reflect the fact that background V0s are expected to have larger values of both

Figure 5.1: The decay topology of a V0. DCA is an acronym for distance of closest approach.

variables compared real V0s. In principle, both these cuts should be zero, however the finite TPC spatial resolution leads to the requirement of a finite cut. Finally, as illustrated in figure 5.1, the decay kinematics result in the V0 daughters typically pointing away from the vertex. As this will be in contrast to primary tracks, cuts on the distance of closest approach of the daughters to the primary vertex should again reject random track crossings. At higher \(p_{T}\), theses cuts are loosened as the V0 tracks are more straight which in turn leads to them pointing more towards the vertex.

To enable particle identification, the \(\Lambda\), \(\bar{\Lambda}\), and \(K^{0}_{S}\) invariant mass hypotheses can be applied to each V0 candidate. The calculation of the \(\Lambda\) invariant mass is shown in the following equation:

\[M_{\Lambda}=M_{p}+M_{\pi}+2(E_{p}E_{\pi}-\vec{p_{p}}\cdot\vec{p_{\pi}}) \tag{5.4}\]

where \(M\) is the particle rest mass, \(E\) is the particle energy and \(p\) is the particle momentum vector. The momentum terms are the measured values for the positive and negative track, the energy terms are calculated assuming the positive track is a proton and the negative track is a pion. The equation arises out of the assumption of momentum and energy conservation. Similar calculations are made for the \(\bar{\Lambda}\), and \(K^{0}_{S}\) invariant mass hypotheses. Figure 5.2 shows each invariant mass for a set

